Hi there!  One of the lang designers here.<p>That&#x27;s been part and parcel for C# for over 10 years at this point.  When we added `?.` originally, it was its nature that it would not execute code that was now unnecessary due to the receiver being null.  For example:<p><pre><code>    Settings?.SetRetryPolicy(new ExponentialBackoffRetryPolicy());
</code></pre>
This would already <i>not</i> run anything on the RHS of the `?.` if `Settings` was null.<p>So this feature behaves consistently with how the language has always treated this space.  Except now it doesn&#x27;t have an artificial limitation on which &#x27;expression&#x27; level it stops at.
